[Event "Chapter 1: The Exchange Variation (Part 1) / Study 1: White Plays 8.Be3 and 8.Bb5+ / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/87ec53e9-9572-4fe2-8f24-c51d0b9e3983/chapters/93d04c58-4346-406e-be8e-bc2ffcec5136/studies/da1440b3-d54a-40ed-980e-fc66a12e337c/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to our course on the Grünfeld Defense! It is a very specific opening where we play in King's Indian style but allow White to place both pawns in the center without playing ...d6. Instead, we play ...d5 eventually. Let's begin. White opens with 1.d4 and we'll reply with Nf6.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {They will most likely place another pawn in the center. And we can continue King's Indian style. Our bishop is coming to g7.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White getting the knight behind the c-pawn happens 75% of the time. However, once White plays Nc3, we won't continue with the standard ...d6. Instead, we will strike in the center right away with d5. This is the Grünfeld Defense!} 3...d5 4.cxd5 {So White captures here 35% of the time. Of course, they don't have to do this, which we will analyze in other chapters. We'll take back with the knight. Let's see how White can approach the position now.} 4...Nxd5 5.e4 {By far the most common move in this position is e4, played almost 80% of the time! White claims that our knight in the center is vulnerable and that we simply don't have any pawns in the center, which allows them to dominate it. We'll capture on c3. Retreating would just allow White to continue developing without any trouble.} 5...Nxc3 6.bxc3 {White recaptures, and it seems like their center is extremely strong. But the main idea of the Grünfeld is to attack on the flank. First, we will activate the bishop on g7.} 6...Bg7 7.Nf3 {Once the knight comes out, here's the key move to remember. It's ...c5! This move is a must to undermine White's structure. Suddenly, it doesn't look so stable anymore as they can neither take on c5 nor push d5 because c3 would be hanging.} 7...c5 8.Bb5+ {One alternative to 8.Be3 is White giving a check on b5. It's played 12% of the time. We'll block the check with Nc6. If White captures on c6, no problem: we get the bishop pair. If they castle, it's also not dangerous.} 8...Nc6 9.d5 {The one move that looks scary is d5. White attacks our pinned knight and even gives up the c3-pawn for it! It's important not to rush here with ...Bxc3: we are better off playing ...Qa5. We target the bishop which is pinning the knight, and the queen provides additional pressure on the c3-pawn.} 9...Qa5 10.Rb1 {White protects the bishop. Now it's time to capture with Bxc3+.} 10...Bxc3+ 11.Bd2 {White blocks with Bd2. Now our bishop is protected by the queen, and that is key. Had we not prepared Bxc3+ in this way, White would have been completely winning after 9...Bxc3+ 10.Bd2 Bxa1 11.Qxa1. In contrast, here we have time to play ...a6 and target the bishop.} 11...a6 12.Bxc6+ {White captures. We'll take back, of course.} 12...bxc6 13.dxc6 {Sure, White can capture on c6.} 13...Be6 {But we will activate the bishop to e6 and the c6-pawn probably won't last for long. Apart from that, we will castle and maintain a lead in activity. We've got an equal position.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Exchange Variation (Part 1) / Study 1: White Plays 8.Be3 and 8.Bb5+ / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/87ec53e9-9572-4fe2-8f24-c51d0b9e3983/chapters/93d04c58-4346-406e-be8e-bc2ffcec5136/studies/da1440b3-d54a-40ed-980e-fc66a12e337c/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to our course on the Grünfeld Defense! It is a very specific opening where we play in King's Indian style but allow White to place both pawns in the center without playing ...d6. Instead, we play ...d5 eventually. Let's begin. White opens with 1.d4 and we'll reply with Nf6.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {They will most likely place another pawn in the center. And we can continue King's Indian style. Our bishop is coming to g7.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White getting the knight behind the c-pawn happens 75% of the time. However, once White plays Nc3, we won't continue with the standard ...d6. Instead, we will strike in the center right away with d5. This is the Grünfeld Defense!} 3...d5 4.cxd5 {So White captures here 35% of the time. Of course, they don't have to do this, which we will analyze in other chapters. We'll take back with the knight. Let's see how White can approach the position now.} 4...Nxd5 5.Nxd5 {Capturing on d5 is only played 5% of the time, and so for a reason. It's usually better for White to take advantage of the center and develop or expand rather than trade. When we recapture with the queen, there is no e4 anymore. And our queen can't be attacked.} 5...Qxd5 6.Nf3 {Instead, White develops with Nf3. We've already got a good position and we can develop comfortably. Of course, it is logical to fianchetto with ...Bg7. The other benefit of it is that we attack the central d4-pawn and basically force White to play e3.} 6...Bg7 7.e3 {This is great for us because the bishop on c1 is now blocked. And we can continue to harass the center. The move ...c5 is extremely common in the Grünfeld, and I suggest you keep it in mind at all times, as it has many applications. Here, the idea is to undermine the center and try to provoke White to capture on c5.} 7...c5 8.Bb5+ {Of course, White capturing on c5 and trading queens would only benefit us. If Be2, we would pile up on the center with ...Nc6 next. That's why Bb5+ makes sense. White wants to develop with tempo and trade off one of our important attackers. We'll block with the knight.} 8...Nc6 9.Bxc6+ {White takes, as planned.} 9...Qxc6 {We'll recapture with the queen. Our plans remain the same and you should never worry about White capturing on c5. Black is better already, with a strong pair of bishops.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Exchange Variation (Part 1) / Study 1: White Plays 8.Be3 and 8.Bb5+ / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/87ec53e9-9572-4fe2-8f24-c51d0b9e3983/chapters/93d04c58-4346-406e-be8e-bc2ffcec5136/studies/da1440b3-d54a-40ed-980e-fc66a12e337c/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to our course on the Grünfeld Defense! It is a very specific opening where we play in King's Indian style but allow White to place both pawns in the center without playing ...d6. Instead, we play ...d5 eventually. Let's begin. White opens with 1.d4 and we'll reply with Nf6.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {They will most likely place another pawn in the center. And we can continue King's Indian style. Our bishop is coming to g7.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White getting the knight behind the c-pawn happens 75% of the time. However, once White plays Nc3, we won't continue with the standard ...d6. Instead, we will strike in the center right away with d5. This is the Grünfeld Defense!} 3...d5 4.cxd5 {So White captures here 35% of the time. Of course, they don't have to do this, which we will analyze in other chapters. We'll take back with the knight. Let's see how White can approach the position now.} 4...Nxd5 5.e4 {By far the most common move in this position is e4, played almost 80% of the time! White claims that our knight in the center is vulnerable and that we simply don't have any pawns in the center, which allows them to dominate it. We'll capture on c3. Retreating would just allow White to continue developing without any trouble.} 5...Nxc3 6.bxc3 {White recaptures, and it seems like their center is extremely strong. But the main idea of the Grünfeld is to attack on the flank. First, we will activate the bishop on g7.} 6...Bg7 7.Nf3 {Once the knight comes out, here's the key move to remember. It's ...c5! This move is a must to undermine White's structure. Suddenly, it doesn't look so stable anymore as they can neither take on c5 nor push d5 because c3 would be hanging.} 7...c5 8.Be3 {Study 1 will deal with 8.Be3 and 8.Bb5+. 8.Be3 is the most common move at amateur level to support d4, and it's played 35% of the time. When challenging any pawn structure, it's important to target the base of it if you can. Therefore we'll play Qa5. Also, the c3-pawn is now pinned so there is more merit to capturing in the center.} 8...Qa5 9.Qd2 {The best move for White is Qd2, protecting both the c3- and the d4-pawn. Now capturing on d4 isn't as strong because White can recapture with the pawn. We will bring the king to safety instead. Next, a good course of action would be to get the knight out to c6 and continue attacking the center.} 9...O-O 10.Be2 {The move that's played 40% of the time is Be2. But it's an inaccuracy! White allows the easy ...Nc6 to attack the center again.} 10...Nc6 11.O-O {Their idea is probably to castle quickly. But now there's a forced sequence whereby we win a pawn. We'll capture on d4.} 11...cxd4 12.cxd4 {The pawn recaptures. Now we'll take the queen.} 12...Qxd2 13.Bxd2 {The bishop or the knight have to recapture. Either way, White loses a central defender.} 13...Nxd4 {I'd say this is an acceptable position out of the opening, how about you? Sure, the queens are off the board, but we have an extra pawn and there's no counterplay for White. Black is much better.} *

