1.d4 {Welcome to our course on the Grünfeld Defense! It is a very specific opening where we play in King's Indian style but allow White to place both pawns in the center without playing ...d6. Instead, we play ...d5 eventually. Let's begin. White opens with 1.d4 and we'll reply with Nf6.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {They will most likely place another pawn in the center. And we can continue King's Indian style. Our bishop is coming to g7.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White getting the knight behind the c-pawn happens 75% of the time. However, once White plays Nc3, we won't continue with the standard ...d6. Instead, we will strike in the center right away with d5. This is the Grünfeld Defense!} 3...d5 4.cxd5 {So White captures here 35% of the time. Of course, they don't have to do this, which we will analyze in other chapters. We'll take back with the knight. Let's see how White can approach the position now.} 4...Nxd5 5.e4 {By far the most common move in this position is e4, played almost 80% of the time! White claims that our knight in the center is vulnerable and that we simply don't have any pawns in the center, which allows them to dominate it. We'll capture on c3. Retreating would just allow White to continue developing without any trouble.} 5...Nxc3 6.bxc3 {White recaptures, and it seems like their center is extremely strong. But the main idea of the Grünfeld is to attack on the flank. First, we will activate the bishop on g7.} 6...Bg7 7.Nf3 {Once the knight comes out, here's the key move to remember. It's ...c5! This move is a must to undermine White's structure. Suddenly, it doesn't look so stable anymore as they can neither take on c5 nor push d5 because c3 would be hanging.} 7...c5 8.Be3 {Study 1 will deal with 8.Be3 and 8.Bb5+. 8.Be3 is the most common move at amateur level to support d4, and it's played 35% of the time. When challenging any pawn structure, it's important to target the base of it if you can. Therefore we'll play Qa5. Also, the c3-pawn is now pinned so there is more merit to capturing in the center.} 8...Qa5 9.Qd2 {The best move for White is Qd2, protecting both the c3- and the d4-pawn. Now capturing on d4 isn't as strong because White can recapture with the pawn. We will bring the king to safety instead. Next, a good course of action would be to get the knight out to c6 and continue attacking the center.} 9...O-O 10.Bc4 {If White wants to develop the bishop to a more active square, that's fine. We'll continue with the standard plan. With Nc6, we harass the d4-pawn further.} 10...Nc6 11.O-O {If White castles...} 11...Bg4 {Here comes the bishop. It's not going to be easy for White to keep hold of the center. And if they do, we can always capture on f3 and ruin their pawn structure.} *

